>
  <p class="answer-label">Answers</p>
  <h1 class="font-display">Relapse is recovery</h1>

  <p class="answer-lead">
    Binging again — after a good week, a good month, a good year — does not erase your recovery.
    Clinically, relapse is a normal and expected part of recovery, not a failure. That's not a
    comforting slogan; it's how recovery actually moves.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The morning-after voice.</strong> If you're reading this the day after, you probably
    know the voice: <em>you ruined it. You were doing so well and you threw it away. Back to
    zero.</em> That voice feels like accountability. It isn't. It's the same shame that fuels the
    cycle — and it lies about the shape of recovery.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Recovery isn't a straight line — and it was never a streak.</strong> Recovery moves
    like weather: stretches of clear days, then a storm, then clear again. The storm doesn't
    delete the clear days. Nothing about what you built — the urges you rode out, the things you
    learned about your own triggers — disappears because of one night. It's all still yours.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What the streak model gets wrong.</strong> Streaks that reset when you slip are
    clinically backwards for binge eating recovery. An app that resets everything you built when
    you slip becomes something you dread opening precisely when you need it most. That dread —
    hiding from your own support the morning after — is the opposite of recovery. Whatever
    support you use should be <em>easiest</em> to face on the hard mornings, not hardest.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The next kind thing.</strong> You don't have to fix everything today. You especially
    don't have to punish yourself back on track — being harder on yourself today is the thing
    that tends to make tomorrow worse, not better. The most useful move after a binge is usually
    the smallest one: say what happened somewhere it won't be judged. To a friend, a therapist, a
    companion like Kippi. And if this morning feels darker than shame — if you're not sure you're
    safe — please reach out to a crisis line right now; there's one at the bottom of this page.
    "I just binged" is a first-class door, not a confession box. Walking through it — instead of
    going silent for a week — is what tends to make the next wave smaller.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">Common questions</h2>

  <h3>Did I ruin my recovery by binging?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Relapse is a normal and expected part of recovery — not a failure. Everything you built
    before last night still counts: the urges you rode out, what you've learned about your
    triggers, every hard morning you've already survived. One night doesn't reset any of it.
  </p>

  <h3>Why do recovery streaks backfire?</h3>
  <p>
    Streaks that reset when you slip are clinically backwards for binge eating recovery. An app
    that resets everything you built when you slip becomes something you dread opening precisely
    when you need it most — so the support disappears exactly when it matters.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">Where Kippi fits</h2>

  <div class="kippi-fit">
    <p>
      "Relapse is recovery" is the idea Kippi is built on. There are no streaks and nothing
      resets — if the first thing you want to say is "I just binged," that is a first-class door,
      not a confession box. Kippi is there before, during, or after, with no food logging, no
      calorie tracking, and no judgment. Try the web version at
